 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The algorithm processes each cube independently. The complexity per cube is dominated by the interior test and convex hull construction, which are constant time for a fixed number of vertices (8) and edges (12). Thus, it is $O(N)$ for $N$ voxels.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the output mesh. The algorithm requires minimal auxiliary memory per cube (labels, groups, hull points).
-- **Measured Performance:** In a 5x5x5 piecewise-trilinear field test, the method produced correct Betti numbers (b0=1, b1=0, b2=1), whereas the Raman and Wenger method (which uses a lookup table covering only 23 of 33 behaviors) produced incorrect Betti numbers (b0=4, b1=0, b2=4) [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Guarantees topological correctness for all 33 cases of the trilinear interpolant; avoids degenerate and skinny triangles; handles the "=" label explicitly.
-- **Limitations:** More complex implementation than standard Marching Cubes due to the need for group identification and interior testing.
-- **When to use:** When topological correctness and mesh quality (avoiding degenerate triangles) are critical, and the underlying scalar field is assumed to be interpolated by a trilinear function.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Higher than standard Marching Cubes due to the interior test and convex hull construction per ambiguous cell. The interior test is $O(1)$ but involves evaluating the trilinear interpolant at specific points. Convex hull construction is $O(N \log N)$ where $N$ is the number of points in the group (typically small, $\le 8$).
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ per cell for local data structures. Global face tracking requires $O(F)$ memory where $F$ is the number of faces in the grid.
+*   **Performance:** Produces topologically correct meshes with correct Betti numbers, unlike Raman and Wenger's method which fails for ambiguous configurations like Case 10 [S1]. In a 5x5x5 test, it produced correct Betti numbers (1, 0, 1) compared to Raman and Wenger's (4, 0, 4) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles the "=" label to avoid degenerate triangles; extends Marching Cubes 33 to cover all 33 topological cases; produces high-quality triangles.
+*   **Limitations:** More complex implementation than standard MC; requires careful handling of face deduplication; interior test adds computational overhead.
 
 ## Background
-The base Marching Cubes algorithm was proposed by Lorensen and Cline in 1987 [S1]. Marching Cubes 33, which guarantees topological correctness for the trilinear interpolant, was proposed by Chernyaev in 1995 [S1]. The concept of an extended lookup table including "+" and "-" labels was introduced by Raman and Wenger in 2008, but their table only covered 23 of the 33 possible behaviors, leading to topological errors in ambiguous cases like Case 10 [S1]. Extended Trilinear Triangulation extends the Marching Cubes 33 table to handle the "=" label and correctly resolves all 33 cases using convex hulls and interior tests, as presented in the source paper [S1].
+*   **Origin:** The base Marching Cubes algorithm was proposed by Lorensen and Cline in 1987 [S1]. Marching Cubes 33 was proposed by Chernyaev in 1995 to address topological ambiguities [S1]. The extended lookup table concept was introduced by Raman and Wenger in 2008, but their method only covered 23 of 33 cases [S1]. Extended Trilinear Triangulation extends the topologically correct Marching Cubes 33 table to handle the "=" label, preserving topology while avoiding degenerate triangles [S1].
+*   **History:** The method was developed to address the lingering problems of triangle quality and topology correctness in Marching Cubes. It builds on the work of Montani et al. (1994) and Chernyaev (1995), improving upon Raman and Wenger's (2008) extended lookup table by covering all 33 cases [S1].
 
